  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nClouds will begin to clear by late Friday afternoon through the \novernight hours just as a surface high begins to settle over the FA \nand drops winds to 5 mph or less. This will be a decent setup for \nradiational cooling. Dewpoints behind the front will drop into the \n20s on the Caprock and low/mid 30s off the Caprock. There is some \nuncertainty with how low temps will drop by Saturday morning. As of \nnow, there is high confidence in at least our northwestern zones \nhitting at to below freezing. Further south and east, temperatures \nwill be very dependent upon cloud cover and where the surface low \neventually settles. The NBM has started to come around to most MOS \nguidance. MOS guidance (MAV, MET, and ECM) have been pointing to \nlows at least in the mid 30s across the Caprock for several days now \nand continue to lower with each run. MET guidance is the coolest at \n31 at KLBB. While the forecast grids don't reflect the coolest, they \nhave been lowered from NBM. A Freeze Watch will also be in effect \nfor areas on the Caprock from 06-15Z Saturday. While not all of the \narea may see freezing temps, they should get low enough to cause \nstress to sensitive plants, especially those still taking advantage \nof the semi-late growing season and recent rainfall. We are also \nseveral days from the average first freeze across the northwest and \ntwo weeks before at Lubbock.\n\nUpper ridging will move overhead this weekend as part of a weak \nblocking pattern, and the upper high will push to the southeast by \nSaturday afternoon. The shifting surface high will bring surface \nwinds to the south to southeast and will help to warm highs back \ninto the 70s early next week. Another upper is progged by models to \nmove into California by mid next week, but there is much \ndisagreement in the models with the future of the upper low beyond \nWednesday.",
